hey this is presh tallwalker  a square is tilted between the ground  and two sets of objects  the left side is supported by a square  whose side length is equal to 1.5 and  its right side is supported by three  stacked squares with side lengths of one  two and three going from the top  the corner of the top square is  coincident with the right corner of the  blue tilted square and the squares of  side lengths of 2 and 3 are aligned on  the right side  the question is what is the area of the  blue tilted square  this problem is adapted from one from at  seashare41  on twitter  pause the video if you'd like to give  this problem a try and when you're ready  keep watching to learn how to solve this  problem  do  [Music]  a tricky part of this puzzle is there's  too much information  you don't need the square whose side  length is equal to 1.5 at all  instead let's zoom in on the squares  whose side lengths are equal to one two  and three  the middle square has a side length  equal to two  thus the remaining distance between the  corner of the middle square and the  upper corner of the bottom square is  equal to three minus two which is equal  to one  now focus on this right triangle  its short leg is equal to one and its  longer leg is equal to the side of the  middle square which is equal to two  let's now consider the following right  triangle which will be similar to this  right triangle  we know that its longer leg will be  equal to the sum of the lengths of the  three squares  so this will be equal to one plus two  plus three which is equal to six  the shorter leg must therefore be one  half of six which is equal to three  let's now focus on just this right  triangle  we know its legs are equal to six and  three  and its hypotenuse let's say it's equal  to x  x is also the side length of the square  the area of the square is equal to x  squared  and that's equal to 3 squared plus 6  squared  thus x squared is equal to 45  and that's the answer  thanks for making u